          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Salmorejo is one of Andalusia's proudest dishes. Cordoba considers it its own, and the rivalry with Seville's gazpacho is a point of local pride. The key difference is the bread, which gives salmorejo its thick, velvety texture. Before tomatoes arrived from the Americas, the soup was made with just bread and herbs. The modern version dates from the 19th century."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
